@@s001 Model complexity
@@s002 dato:training (years 0–7),due:test (years 7–10),ok:degree 1,ora:degree 3,no:degree 6
@@s003 The exercise on trees (week 7, <i>model complexity</i>). 70 growth measurements for the <b>training</b> (years 0–7) and 30 measurements of another group for the <b>test</b> (years 7–10). The test comes <b>after</b> the training: the model must predict the future.
@@s004 <b>Degree 1</b> (a line, 2 parameters). Training MSE <b>0.797</b>, test <b>0.857</b>: it errs little even on the years it has not seen.
@@s005 <b>Degree 3</b>. The training falls to <b>0.731</b>, the test rises to <b>1.609</b>: the curve bends to follow the training points better.
@@s006 <b>Degree 6</b>. Training <b>0.720</b>, the lowest. Test <b>808.6</b>: after year 8 the curve plunges and leaves the chart. Outside the training data a high-degree polynomial has nothing to hold it.
@@s007 The data were generated by a <b>line</b>, 1.5x + 0.2, plus noise. The simplest model was the right one: the extra degrees learned only the noise. Training error ever lower, test error not: it is <b>overfitting</b>.
@@s008 dato:training,ora:test,no:degree 1,ok:degree 3,due:degree 9
@@s009 dato:training RMSE,ora:test RMSE,ok:best order
@@s010 no:bias²,due:variance,spento:σ² (irreducible noise)
@@s011 no:underfitting,ok:Occam's choice,due:to verify with cross-validation
@@s012 Model complexity
@@s013 Week 7, exercises <i>Polynomial regression</i> and <i>Model complexity and overfitting</i>; exam question <b>Q5</b>. A more flexible model always errs less on the training data, but it does not therefore predict better. Here you see why, with the real numbers of the exercises. DW chapter 11.
@@s014 Degree of the polynomial = complexity
@@s015 A polynomial of degree d has <b>d + 1 parameters</b> (<a href="w04-polinomi.html">polynomials</a>): more degree, more parameters, more freedom to bend. The method to learn it does not change (design matrix [1, x, …, xᵈ] and least squares). What changes is how much the model manages to chase the data, including their noise.
@@s016 Degrees 1, 3 and 6 on the trees (seed 42 of the notebook)
@@s017 Data and MSE obtained by running the code of the exercise (<code>np.random.seed(42)</code>, <code>PolynomialFeatures</code> + <code>LinearRegression</code>).
@@s018 Degree
@@s019 MSE training
@@s020 MSE test
@@s021 The two rules to remember
@@s022 (1) The <b>training</b> error always falls (or stays the same) as the degree increases: the bigger model contains the smaller one. (2) The error on <b>new</b> data does not: first it falls, then it rises. One chooses the model by looking at the second.
@@s023 Underfitting and overfitting
@@s024 Underfitting
@@s025 Model too <b>simple</b> for the shape of the data: high error on the training <b>and</b> on the test. Example: a line on data that grow like a parabola (Task 5, below): training MSE 16.3, test 567.6.
@@s026 Right
@@s027 It catches the trend, leaves out the noise: training and test errors similar and low.
@@s028 Overfitting
@@s029 Model too <b>flexible</b>: very low training error, high test error. It has learned the data, noise included, instead of the function. Degree 6 of the trees.
@@s030 How to recognise it from the numbers
@@s031 Underfitting: better features or a more flexible model are needed.
@@s032 Overfitting: the gap between the two (generalisation error) is the signal.
@@s033 The model generalises.
@@s034 Degrees 1, 3, 9 on training and test
@@s035 The Windsor houses: the U-shaped curve
@@s036 Exercise <i>Polynomial regression</i>: price of the houses (546) as a function of the lot area, random split 80/20, RMSE in Canadian dollars.
@@s037 Order
@@s038 RMSE training
@@s039 RMSE test
@@s040 Computed with the data of the notebook (<code>simple_windsor.csv</code>) and its normalisation. The training always falls; the test has its minimum at orders 5–6 and then rises. The differences are small compared with the RMSE (about 22 000 $): the lot explains only a part of the price.
@@s041 Tasks 5 and 7: why from degree 7 the predictions go crazy
@@s042 The columns of the design matrix have very distant magnitudes (1, 16 200, 2.6 · 10⁸, …).
@@s043 condition number: order 7 → <mark class="no">1.1 · 10³²</mark>, order 11 → <mark class="no">5.6 · 10⁴⁷</mark>
@@s044 A computer keeps about 16 significant digits: beyond 10¹⁶ least squares returns meaningless weights.
@@s045 The proof that it is a numerical problem: a bigger model cannot err more on the training (rule 1 above). Here it happens because the calculations are broken.
@@s046 The powers of numbers smaller than 1 stay small: the matrix becomes manageable again and the training falls again with the order. Normalisation fixes the <b>calculations</b>, not the model. (In the notebook one divides by the maximum instead of by max − min: the values end up between 0 and 0.9, which is fine all the same.)
@@s047 Training falling, test U-shaped
@@s048 Bias and variance
@@s049 Err = Bias² + Variance + σ²  (DW eq. 11.1)
@@s050 <mark class="ora">Bias²</mark>: how far the <b>average</b> prediction is from the true function
@@s051 Systematic error. It is produced by a model too rigid: even with infinitely many training data it would stay wrong. In the book's target: shots all close together but off centre.
@@s052 <mark class="due">Variance</mark>: how much the model changes if you change the training data
@@s053 A flexible model adapts to the noise of <b>that</b> sample: with another sample another curve comes out. In the target: scattered shots.
@@s054 The noise of the data: no model removes it. It is the minimum possible error on new data.
@@s055 Degree
@@s056 MSE training
@@s057 Bias²
@@s058 Variance
@@s059 Sum
@@s060 MSE on new data
@@s061 Simulation (NumPy): true function sin(2πx), 12 training points, noise σ = 0.3, repeated with 5000 different noise samples. The sum of the three terms coincides with the error measured on new data: the decomposition works. Degree 1 is all bias, degree 9 all variance; the best lies in between. Note the training of degree 9: 0.015, <b>below</b> σ² = 0.09. It has learned the noise.
@@s062 Bias² + variance + σ²
@@s063 Occam's razor (Task 5)
@@s064 Occam: among models that explain the data <b>equally well</b>, choose the simplest. It does not mean "always choose the simplest".
@@s065 Same experiment, true function x² + 1.5x − 3
@@s066 The line cannot follow a parabola: underfitting. The simplest of all is wrong here.
@@s067 The simplest that explains the data: it is Occam's choice.
@@s068 Here the test of degree 3 is lower, but with a single split of 30 points, all in the future, the difference can be chance. To decide reliably one needs <a href="w07-validazione.html">cross-validation</a>.
@@s069 Q5 (b) and (c)
@@s070 To be able to discuss: data quality and noise → how much a complex model can "believe" the data; underfitting and overfitting recognised from the training and test numbers; bias and variance; why one evaluates on unseen data (train/test, cross-validation) and what happens when extrapolating outside the range of the training data.
@@s071 Ready sentences
@@s072 "Training error always decreases with model complexity, test error does not: the gap between them signals overfitting."
@@s073 "A high-degree polynomial has low bias but high variance: it fits the noise of the particular training sample."
@@s074 The simplest that explains the data
